so talk to me about what inspired you to  make this documentary you're talking  about somebody who's been dead for  thirty-three years who in many ways was  a very New York  / East Coast phenomenon but now suddenly  makes more sense yes well it came out of  a previous project I was making a movie  called Studio 54  Roy Cohn was the lawyer for Studio 54 so  all through the election year of 2016  I'm watching work own footage in that  context and I keep thinking to myself  this is a great subject for a movie and  he's leaping off the screen he's a great  character and I knew about his  relationship to Trump but then I would  think that and put it out in my head  because I thought well Hillary Clinton's  going to win the election and there'll  be nothing new to say about Roy Cohn and  then on imagine that yes and a night in  early November election night was in a  hotel room in New York actually and then  things went sideways the first thing I  did was go outside and get a huge ice  cream sundae the second thing was I  drank all the wine in the room and the  third thing I did was write the  treatment for this film because I really  thought it was an urgent story to tell  suddenly what we had because I've been  thinking about it before we had someone  Roy Cohn who would have been a bold  footnote to American history who  overnight became a modern Machiavelli  so that story I thought needed to be  told urgently as soon as possible and  here we are and of course you know in  this and of course you you cover the  fact that there are some very deep  historical roots to this this  Machiavellian structure that this man  put into action but let's talk about the  very very origins there I mean what more  the first thing you discover when you  started drilling into this this subject  little more deeply than just the initial  studio 54 footage well you know it goes  way back so you have someone born in the  20s who is a prodigy  and really brilliant person that turns  out but he's kind of a dark spirit from  very early on incredibly ambitious and  he uses his intelligence in a really  deadly way and becomes a part of a  demagogic movement that was transpiring  when he was in his early twenties we  call it the McCarthy era but you know  it's the catch-all for the persecution  of the other basically and in that  period it was communists or Bolsheviks  or pink ladies homosexuals as well you  know there were these shadowy others  that demagogue politicians used to gain  power and Cohen attaches himself to  people such as Jerry Hoover and of  course ultimately Joseph McCarthy it's  part of what the historian Richard  Hofstadter labelled the paranoid style  of American politics and Roy Cohn who is  I believe our modern Machiavelli  posthumously was the great manipulator  of that and he's really teaching  McCarthy how to maximize his  effectiveness in a new era of  communications which was the television  era so now we're starting to get into  oblique things and resonances that  should I believe remind us of current  politics but I thought was really  interesting to go back and without any  overt commentary just lay that all out  so we who live in what Gore V doll  called the United States of amnesia can  better remember how we got to now and  this movie is how we got to now dot  connecting attempt so much of directing  the documentaries about editing and  other post decisions can you talk about  some of the choices you made here both  in terms of animation and the editing  style yeah I mean this is an archival  film with interviews I have another  genre of doc I do which is cinema verite  very different set of muscles because  the persons with you you'll basically  move in with someone and have  the strange transference that's a very  particular thing here you're kind of  building a case in a way it's more si  istic and of course it has to be visual  so what's it going to be well I knew  what I wanted at the outset I had a kind  of film noir a color film noir in a way  tone palette in my mind and even down to  musical choices I had an idea and then I  started to kind of try things out  we found extraordinary footage and we  went through a great deal of effort to  get for instance footage from 60 minutes  that was shot in the 60s and 70s that no  one's seen before and that was its film  so it's really beautiful when you see it  on screen another thing that we heard  was that Roy Cohn's personal archive had  been bought at an auction not long ago  by an anonymous buyer and it turned out  to be a true rumor and I tracked down  the person it's actually someone I knew  who asked to remain anonymous and he  they gave us the photos so what you're  seeing when you see shirtless boys  frolicking on yachts are Roy Cohn's  photos that no one's ever seen before  and surely he did not want you to see  those photos so that's helped for the  elements in terms of the way the  graphics structure is very intricately  organized it has a sort of reportorial  investigative thrust to it and  everything is built like that so you  have tape recorders whirring and that  was created in our studio and then the  graphics are all through the lens of  microfiche so they're reversed you're  seeing them in the negative as you would  microfilm so there's sort of like a an  unprecendented a propulsive nature and  kind of bring the audience through thank  you very much Matt appreciate your  comments
 

